# Code Modification Standards

## Context
- When modifying existing, working code
- When adding new features to existing systems
- When attempting to improve or optimize code
- When fixing bugs or addressing issues

## Requirements

### General Principles
- Do not modify working code without clear requirements
- Document the reason for any significant changes
- Preserve existing behavior unless explicitly required to change it
- Keep changes minimal and focused
- Test changes against existing use cases

### Before Modifying
- Understand why the current implementation works
- Document the specific problem being solved
- Verify the change is actually necessary
- Consider the impact on existing consumers
- Check if the change belongs at a different layer

### During Implementation
- Make incremental changes
- Maintain existing patterns where possible
- Add clear documentation for changes
- Preserve existing interfaces
- Add new paths rather than modifying existing ones

### After Changes
- Verify all existing use cases still work
- Document any behavioral changes
- Update tests to cover new cases
- Review for unnecessary complexity
- Validate performance impact

## Examples

<example>
```rust
// Good: Adding new functionality without modifying existing
struct DataProcessor {
    // ... existing fields ...
}

impl DataProcessor {
    // Existing method preserved
    pub fn process(&self, data: &[u8]) -> Result<Output> {
        // ... existing implementation ...
    }
    
    // New method added instead of modifying existing
    pub fn process_with_options(&self, data: &[u8], opts: Options) -> Result<Output> {
        if opts.use_legacy {
            self.process(data)
        } else {
            // New implementation
            self.process_new(data, opts)
        }
    }
}
```
Adds new functionality while preserving existing behavior
</example>

<example type="invalid">
```rust
// Bad: Modifying working code without clear need
impl InputHandler {
    pub fn handle_input(&self, data: Vec<u8>) -> Result<()> {
        // Original working code modified without requirement
        let processed = data.iter()
            .map(|b| b.reverse_bits())  // Why was this added?
            .collect::<Vec<_>>();
        
        self.process_data(&processed)
    }
}
```
Changes existing behavior without clear necessity
</example>

<example>
```rust
// Good: Clear documentation of necessary changes
/// Updates user authentication to support new MFA requirements
/// Change required by: SEC-123 - MFA Compliance Update
impl Authenticator {
    pub fn authenticate(&self, credentials: &Credentials) -> Result<Session> {
        // First verify existing credentials
        self.verify_credentials(credentials)?;
        
        // Then add new MFA check
        if self.mfa_required(credentials.user_id) {
            self.verify_mfa(credentials)?;
        }
        
        self.create_session(credentials)
    }
}
```
Documents why changes were needed and maintains compatibility
</example>

<example type="invalid">
```rust
// Bad: Rewriting working code without necessity
pub fn parse_config(data: &str) -> Config {
    // Original simple working code:
    // serde_json::from_str(data).unwrap()
    
    // Unnecessary complex rewrite:
    let mut lines = data.lines();
    let mut config = Config::default();
    while let Some(line) = lines.next() {
        // ... complex manual parsing ...
    }
    config
}
```
Replaces simple working solution with unnecessary complexity
</example> 